course: dictionary
lesson: 12005
title: Cheekily
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Cheekily tells you how someone did something. It is an adverb, so it often comes after a verb, like «answered cheekily» or «grinned cheekily». Ask yourself: how did they do it? The answer is cheekily.
explain: The word describes behaviour that is a little rude but often light-hearted. «Mia winked cheekily at her brother when Mum was not looking.» Mia is not being cruel. The wink and the secret look show she is playful and a bit naughty.
explain: Look at the words around it. «"Can I have another biscuit?" Tom asked cheekily, with a grin.» The grin tells you Tom is being bold and playful, not angry. Try your meaning in the sentence, such as "in a playful, slightly naughty way", and check it still makes sense.
fact: Cheekily means in a slightly rude but often funny or playful way.
fact: Cheekily is an adverb, so it tells you how something is done.
fact: Cheekily can also mean in a bold, playful way, as in «a cheeky grin».
fact: The adjective is cheeky, and the comparative form is more cheekily.
fact: Cheekily is especially used in the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand.
exercise: «Lily grinned cheekily when she got the last biscuit.» How did Lily grin? | Angrily, with her arms folded | Sadly and slowly | *In a playful, slightly naughty way
exercise: «The puppy looked cheekily at the sofa.» What does cheekily tell us? | *How the puppy looked | What the sofa is made of | The colour of the puppy
exercise: «Max answered cheekily, "Do I have to?"» Which words show that Max is being playful, not angry? | *His words and the playful way he answered | The clock on the wall | His shoes and his coat | The weather outside the window
test: «The boy answered his teacher cheekily.» Which meaning fits best? | *In a slightly rude but playful way | With lots of money | In a very sad and quiet way | In a frightened, shaking way
test: Ella says, «Cheekily means rude and mean.» Is she right? | Yes, it always means cruel and mean | Yes, it means the same as angrily | *No, but it can be a little rude in a playful way, and it is never cruel | No, it is usually light-hearted and playful, not cruel
exam: «The fox grinned cheekily at the hens.» What does cheekily suggest about the fox? | It is hurt and crying | It is asleep and very tired | *It is playful and a bit naughty | It is hungry and lost in the woods
